--------------------------------------------------------------------------
[4] explore_multipliers.py.  Stripping the shared falling-factorial and
    denominator-product structure gives an r,b-INDEPENDENT multiplier M_p(k)
    (first run p<=6,r<=16,b<=5: 4494 instances, 0 inconsistencies; the retained
    log is the wider rerun p<=7,r<=18,b<=5: 6264 instances, 0).  Table:

        p\k    0     1     2     3      4      5
        0      1     1     1     1      1      1
        1      1     3     6    10     15     21
        2      2    11    35    85    175    322
        3      6    50   225   735   1960   4536
        4     24   274  1624  6769  22449  63273
        5    120  1764 13132 67284 269325 902055

    Recognised immediately:  column k=0 is p! ; column k=1 is 1, 3, 11, 50,
    274, 1764 = (p+1)! H_{p+1}.  Those are c(p+1,1) and c(p+2,2), the
    unsigned Stirling numbers of the first kind.  CONJECTURE:

        M_p(k) = c(k+p+1, k+1)

    which reproduces the three PROVED multipliers exactly, since
    c(N,N)=1, c(N,N-1)=C(N,2), c(N,N-2)=(3N-1)/4 C(N,3).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
[5] THE KEY OBSERVATION, by hand.  If the multiplier is c(j+1,k+1) with
    j := k+p, then at fixed j the eps/t part of the expansion is

      sum_k c(j+1,k+1) t^k eps^(j-k) = (1/t) sum_M c(j+1,M) t^M eps^(j+1-M)
                                     = (1/t) * t(t+eps)...(t+j eps)
                                     = prod_{i=1}^{j} (t + i eps),

    the homogenised rising-factorial generating identity for c(n,m).  And
    prod_{i=1}^{j}(t + i/n) = (m+j)!/(m! n^j) exactly.  So the whole series
    RESUMS, and because it terminates at p=r the resummation is EXACT:

      g_r(m,b) = sum_{j=0}^{r} c_j^(r)(b) * (m+j)!/(m! n^j).

    Hand-checked at r=0,1,2 before coding: reproduces g_0=1/(b+1),
    psi_n^(1)=(4n+1)/(6n), psi_n^(2)=(8n^2+4n+1)/(15n^2) exactly, and
    psi_n^(3)=(64n^3+48n^2+25n+6)/(140n^3) after one page of arithmetic.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
[6] BY HAND: the proof.  Writing A_j := c_j^(r)(b) and P_j(m) := (m+j)!/m!,
    the two elementary facts P_j(m)-P_j(m-1) = j P_{j-1}(m) and
    P_j(m-1) = m P_{j-1}(m), together with

        A_j (j+1+r+b) = r A_{j-1}^(r-1)(b+1)     and   (1+r+b) A_0 = 1,

    turn m[ghat_r(m)-ghat_r(m-1)] + (1+r+b) ghat_r(m-1) into exactly
    1 + (r m/n) ghat_{r-1}(m,b+1), which is 1 + r hhat_{r-1}(n-m+1,b) once
    hhat is DEFINED as ((n-a+1)/n) ghat_r(n-a+1,b+1).  The source rule is the
    same identity re-indexed.  Uniqueness of the solution of the transition
    system is immediate (a+b strictly increases on every call, bounded by n),
    so no separate base case or boundary layer is needed anywhere.

